Dr. Karan Mittal: Yes, we should move.

332
00:57:00.360 --> 00:57:03.070
Dr. Karan Mittal: So, we'll come to a few biases.

333
00:57:03.320 --> 00:57:12.120
Dr. Karan Mittal: And, of course, we'll… because it's more of a bias concept, we'll not go very deep, but… but how these biases affect us. Like, confirmation biases.

334
00:57:12.390 --> 00:57:26.419
Dr. Karan Mittal: something that you have already believed in your mind to exist, and you start imposing that. It happens a lot with, you know, relationship counseling. Suppose I see the wife first in my counseling session, and wife creates a narrative about the husband.

335
00:57:26.620 --> 00:57:34.760
Dr. Karan Mittal: the session… probably the husband joins therapy a few sessions later. I will be seeing the husband from the perspective the wife has created in me.

336
00:57:35.150 --> 00:57:52.890
Dr. Karan Mittal: So, what is very important is, like, suppose someone is referring you a client, and he says that this guy, your problem is, this is this client's problem, etc, etc. And you kind of take in that narrative and kind of imbibe it within yourself, and when that client or the counselor comes to you, you will be just

337
00:57:52.890 --> 00:57:58.680
Dr. Karan Mittal: Working from that narrative and seeing those small, smaller things that confirm that narrative within you.

338
00:57:58.680 --> 00:58:17.999
Dr. Karan Mittal: So it's very important that, you know, in therapy also, what I do many times is, suppose a client is taking therapy for a few sessions, there's a gap of two, three weeks, and then traveling or some family function, and the client resumes in the fourth week. The new session… the fourth… the session that's happening in the fourth week, for me as a therapist, is a new session.

339
00:58:18.220 --> 00:58:38.129
Dr. Karan Mittal: I keep all the past data separate, I again ask the client what's happening, what are the issues, for a few minutes, so that I get new data, and I just don't work on the confirmation that I did in session one, and continue it further. So it's very important to become that empty container, and every time try to fill in the new information that you're getting. So that's confirmation bias.

340
00:58:38.200 --> 00:58:42.500
Dr. Karan Mittal: And the antidote I've just written, you kind of gather also those

341
00:58:42.590 --> 00:58:52.280
Dr. Karan Mittal: Traits of the client that disconfirm your analysis and diagnosis, and then decide for yourself which part holds strong.

342
00:58:53.190 --> 00:58:54.190
Dr. Karan Mittal: Rebuji.

343
00:58:56.620 --> 00:59:01.380
