01:01:34.860 --> 01:01:36.270
Govind Das: Immediately.

370
01:01:36.710 --> 01:01:45.709
Govind Das: The bias comes there, and they are seen as sinful, they are seen as local, they are seen as untouchable.

371
01:01:46.540 --> 01:01:56.909
Govind Das: Right? Even though scripture is more evolving in regards to being from a non-vegetarian to vegetarian, they are not contradictory concepts.

372
01:01:57.200 --> 01:02:00.730
Govind Das: Their evolving concept. That is a different way to look.

373
01:02:01.270 --> 01:02:02.200
Govind Das: Yes, sir.

374
01:02:03.890 --> 01:02:04.780
Dr. Karan Mittal: Yes, Christian.

375
01:02:05.420 --> 01:02:06.620
Dr. Karan Mittal: modules.

376
01:02:07.940 --> 01:02:23.789
Dr. Karan Mittal: Okay, so… so, cultural and moral bias, also, if you're into the spiritual counseling realm, these are the biases that take over a lot, okay? If you decide that if a person is not doing a particular spiritual practice, then he's good for nothing.

377
01:02:23.820 --> 01:02:48.720
Dr. Karan Mittal: And suppose a client comes to you and shares this issue, etc. Somewhere or the other, unconsciously, subconsciously, you are creating a belief that he's not serious on the spiritual saddler, and that's why he has this problem, etc. So, cultural bias is not just about the childhood experiences, etc. It's also, if you belong to a particular culture, cult, sect, how much of those beliefs do you try to impose on the other client? And that happens very common. Sometimes we don't

378
01:02:48.720 --> 01:02:51.100
Dr. Karan Mittal: Don't even realize that we're doing that.

379
01:02:51.100 --> 01:03:02.940
Dr. Karan Mittal: Okay, someone is not wearing clothes of a particular way, someone is, someone coming from LGBTQ community, live in relationships. These are the things where you have to be really careful, because

380
01:03:02.940 --> 01:03:14.380
Dr. Karan Mittal: Somewhere or the other, your thinking will change, and the way you present the issue or the solutions to the client will also change. Like, for example, Onresh also, you will subconsciously start

381
01:03:14.380 --> 01:03:33.739
Dr. Karan Mittal: judging the client and trying to impose that a vegetarian lifestyle is better. And then you will kind of give reasons to your brain, okay, it reduces the Rajas Guna, it will reduce the aggressiveness, it will reduce the acidity problem, it will do this, it will do that. But it's not these things that you have… you're scanning the client for. You are

382
01:03:33.760 --> 01:03:39.629
Dr. Karan Mittal: working from your bias. So it's very good… important to contemplate that why are you suggesting what you are suggesting.

383
01:03:40.460 --> 01:03:52.000
Dr. Karan Mittal: And moral bias, as we all know, that it's about what's right and wrong. So, LGBTQ and all these things nowadays… Probhji, would you like to add to moral bias? Yeah.

384
01:03:52.000 --> 01:04:01.769
Govind Das: Yeah, again, moral bias is, again, based upon the cultural bias, we create certain, you know, assumed morality. There are universal laws.

385
01:04:01.980 --> 01:04:04.730
Govind Das: Right? Dharma deals with universal laws.

386
01:04:06.410 --> 01:04:12.040
Govind Das: And the morality is also sometimes man-made.

387
01:04:13.110 --> 01:04:14.090
Govind Das: Right?

388
01:04:14.490 --> 01:04:22.519
Govind Das: So in the man-made laws, when they contradict with the universal law, here we focus more on right and wrong.

389
01:04:22.960 --> 01:04:30.060
Govind Das: The universal laws do not focus on right and wrong. Universal laws focus on action and consequences.

390
01:04:31.730 --> 01:04:37.770
Govind Das: if you study how Jatayu spoke to Ravan, It's very interesting.

391
01:04:38.210 --> 01:04:43.350
Govind Das: Jetayu spoke to Ravan from the principle of action and consequences.

392
01:04:44.810 --> 01:04:46.940
Govind Das: He said, you are a king.

393
01:04:47.330 --> 01:04:49.759
Govind Das: People will follow your behavior.

394
01:04:50.410 --> 01:04:56.519
Govind Das: Jatay, you said, you know what, you should only carry the burden as much as you can handle.

395
01:04:57.700 --> 01:05:06.510
Govind Das: So this is very important if you're a therapist with high moral value, which is very important.

396
01:05:06.970 --> 01:05:19.620
Govind Das: But then what does it do? It makes such people unforgivable for even small, slight infringement, because they operate from the principle of

397
01:05:19.900 --> 01:05:21.580
Govind Das: right and wrong.

398
01:05:22.320 --> 01:05:29.510
Govind Das: But the universal morality operates on the principle of Action and consequences.

399
01:05:30.000 --> 01:05:31.990
Govind Das: Big difference between the two.

400
01:05:33.470 --> 01:05:38.810
Govind Das: Right? When you, when you narrate the concept based upon action and consequences.

401
01:05:39.360 --> 01:05:42.530
Govind Das: Then you are giving them the space to think.

402
01:05:43.440 --> 01:05:47.130
Govind Das: But when we are doing based upon right and wrong.

403
01:05:47.670 --> 01:05:53.259
Govind Das: Either they completely submit and not have a conversation.

404
01:05:53.370 --> 01:05:56.780
Govind Das: Or they rebel against and choose their own path.

405
01:05:58.680 --> 01:05:59.540
Govind Das: Correct.

406
01:06:00.460 --> 01:06:01.240
Dr. Karan Mittal: Yes.

407
01:06:01.740 --> 01:06:08.969
Dr. Karan Mittal: And the diagnostic bias, of course, it doesn't… if you're into spiritual counseling, or parenting, or,

408
01:06:08.980 --> 01:06:28.429
Dr. Karan Mittal: education field, it doesn't hold, too much of importance, but it's also, like, the modern psychology is also kind of, you know, debating on this. How important are labels to be put to the client? Anxiety kahe. How important are labels, and are those labels going to

409
01:06:28.710 --> 01:06:41.800
Dr. Karan Mittal: benefit the client, or it will just become a label and a taboo and kind of stick to him for life. So, it doesn't hold too much of importance if you're into general counseling, but as therapists, we always try to kind of not

410
01:06:41.810 --> 01:06:58.019
Dr. Karan Mittal: diagnose the client with a borderline personality disorder, depression, etc. We work around it, we have that framework within ourselves, but we don't… if the client has just come to the therapist and psychiatrist, etc, is not involved, we don't put unnecessary labels to the client.

411
01:06:58.020 --> 01:07:07.259
Dr. Karan Mittal: Okay? And also, we don't talk about, okay, this is because of your father's trauma, this is because of your mother's trauma. We let the client realize it and speak.

412
01:07:07.340 --> 01:07:13.409
Dr. Karan Mittal: Okay? We don't push our understanding on the client, and neither, because then the client will start

413
01:07:13.500 --> 01:07:25.089
Dr. Karan Mittal: analyzing the father from that perspective, even now. So we try to avoid very strong and sticky labels on any, any of the assets, on the client surface. And projection is…

414
01:07:25.310 --> 01:07:42.050
Dr. Karan Mittal: Of course, projection bias also happens very commonly. We try to project our issues, or whatever we have gone through on the client's problems. So, I had this… so suppose I'm giving a very, very extreme example. If the therapist has undergone abuse in his childhood, and he

415
01:07:42.170 --> 01:07:57.229
Dr. Karan Mittal: you know, he kind of… a client comes who has also undergone abuse, then somewhere or the other, the therapist will start projecting the way he dealt with the abuse, the way… the emotions he has, the problem he has right now, on the client. And sometimes this becomes…

416
01:07:57.350 --> 01:08:16.930
Dr. Karan Mittal: too much for the client to handle, because both of them have sailed in different boats. So we try avoiding our… try, you know, avoiding our experiences, whether it's devotional experiences, spiritual experiences, traumatic experiences, on the client. That's why, in psychology also, how much of personal information to share with the client.

417
01:08:16.930 --> 01:08:23.269
Dr. Karan Mittal: It's again a thing of taboo. Nowadays, people are opening up, but until a few years ago, therapists

418
01:08:23.270 --> 01:08:31.569
Dr. Karan Mittal: never shared his personal things with the gift. So that's about the projection bias. So, why are we dealing with these biases? It's because

419
01:08:31.710 --> 01:08:45.540
Dr. Karan Mittal: Next time when you are dealing with a counselor of yours, and you have completed your session, you have completed a few sessions, come back home, take, you know, reflect for 15 minutes, and see which advice came from a bias.

420
01:08:45.540 --> 01:08:58.899
Dr. Karan Mittal: Which analysis came from a bias, which assumption came from a bias, and which was you kind of listening non-judgmentally, and then probably analyzing the client, or being empathetic to client, and then advising.

421
01:08:58.990 --> 01:09:02.589
Dr. Karan Mittal: So that's why knowing your biases is equally important.

422
01:09:02.720 --> 01:09:05.389
Dr. Karan Mittal: And countertransference bias, of course, again.

423
01:09:05.479 --> 01:09:22.089
Dr. Karan Mittal: It happens in cases where you kind of transfer your issues, or your experiences, or something related in your life on the client. So, like, the example I've given, like, if you had a younger brother, and he's probably

424
01:09:22.109 --> 01:09:33.139
Dr. Karan Mittal: are not talking to you, are not able to deal with him, take care of him, etc. And you kind of get a client who's of the same age, going through a similar issue, same looks, etc, who kind of…

425
01:09:33.140 --> 01:09:48.840
Dr. Karan Mittal: feel that the client is your younger brother, and try projecting that feeling of the elder brother on the client. So that's a counter-transparent bias. Freud, Sigmund Freud spoke a lot about it, but not very important unless you are into clinical counseling. But just knowing that, are you projecting any of your issues

426
01:09:48.840 --> 01:09:57.210
Dr. Karan Mittal: Relationships, any of your traumas, etc, on the client, plays an equal important role to become that empty container.

